\section{Setting up the diagnostic quiz, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the diagnostic quiz of \cref{prior-knowledge}: it sets the
quiz up in the learning platform, and it prepares the results for the
analysis.
Two files are tangled from this section: [[quiz.json]], the description
of the quiz that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to Canvas, and
[[analyze_quiz.py]], which summarises the results and prepares the
open-text answers for manual coding.

\subsection{The quiz for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates a quiz from a JSON description:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz.json
\end{minted}
The description has two parts, the quiz-level settings and the list of
questions; the questions in turn are the background items of
\cref{prior-knowledge} followed by the knowledge part.

<<[[quiz.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    <<quiz settings>>
  },
  "items": [
    <<background items>>
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

The quiz is a diagnostic, not an assessment: nothing is graded, so
there is no time limit, a single attempt suffices, and the result view
shows the student their answers but no notion of correctness.

<<quiz settings>>=
"title": "Diagnostic quiz: your programming background",
"instructions": "<p>This quiz maps your programming background so that we can adapt the course. It is not graded and there are no wrong answers. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
"time_limit": null,
"points_possible": 0,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
"quiz_settings": {
  "shuffle_answers": false,
  "shuffle_questions": false,
  "has_time_limit": false,
  "one_at_a_time_type": "none",
  "allow_backtracking": true,
  "multiple_attempts_enabled": false,
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": false,
  "display_points_possible": false
}
@

\subsection{The background items}

The five background items are exactly those motivated in
\cref{prior-knowledge}; the item titles double as the keys under which
Canvas reports the answers, so the analysis program
(\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) refers to the items by these titles.

<<background items>>=
<<item: previous programming>>,
<<item: python>>,
<<item: how learned>>,
<<item: misbehaving program>>,
<<item: confidence>>
@

The first item is open text: which languages, and for how long, cannot
be anticipated as choices.

<<item: previous programming>>=
{
  "position": 1,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Previous programming",
    "item_body": "<p>Have you programmed before this course? Which programming languages have you used, and for roughly how long?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "essay",
    "interaction_data": {"rce": false, "word_limit_enabled": false},
    "scoring_data": {"value": ""},
    "scoring_algorithm": "None"
  }
}
@

The Python item is a yes/no question.
Canvas's true--false question type requires a \enquote{correct} answer
even in an ungraded quiz; the value below is arbitrary and never
shown to the student (the result view above hides correctness).
The same holds for the choice items that follow.

<<item: python>>=
{
  "position": 2,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Python",
    "item_body": "<p>Have you programmed in Python?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "true-false",
    "interaction_data": {"true_choice": "Yes", "false_choice": "No"},
    "scoring_data": {"value": true},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

How the student learned to program is a choice among the three
alternatives of \cref{prior-knowledge}, plus the alternative for those
who have not programmed at all; the order is meaningful, so the choices
are not shuffled (the quiz settings above disable shuffling globally).

<<item: how learned>>=
{
  "position": 3,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "How you learned",
    "item_body": "<p>If you have programmed before: how did you learn&mdash;in a formal course, self-taught, or both?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>In a formal course</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Self-taught</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>Both</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>I have not programmed before this course</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 1},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

The misbehaving-program item is the open-text item whose answers we
code for variation-thinking (\cref{prior-knowledge,mq:agreement}); the
analysis program extracts these answers into a coding sheet.

<<item: misbehaving program>>=
{
  "position": 4,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Misbehaving program",
    "item_body": "<p>When your program misbehaves, what do you do? Describe what you did the last time it happened.</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "essay",
    "interaction_data": {"rce": false, "word_limit_enabled": false},
    "scoring_data": {"value": ""},
    "scoring_algorithm": "None"
  }
}
@

The confidence item is the five-point scale of \cref{prior-knowledge}.

<<item: confidence>>=
{
  "position": 5,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Confidence",
    "item_body": "<p>How confident are you that you could write, run and fix a small program on your own?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>Not at all confident</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Slightly confident</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>Moderately confident</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>Quite confident</p>"},
        {"position": 5, "item_body": "<p>Very confident</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 1},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

\subsection{The knowledge part}

The knowledge part awaits the choice between the SCS1 subset and the
