\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas exports the quiz results as a CSV file with one row per student
and one column per item, keyed by the item titles above.
The analysis program tabulates the closed items---prior programming in
Python, how the student learned, and the confidence scale---and writes
the open-text answers to a coding sheet: a CSV file with one row per
student and empty columns for the codes, to be filled in manually
(\cref{analysis}).

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
"""Summarise the diagnostic quiz results and prepare the coding sheet.

The quiz itself is defined in quiz.json; both files are tangled from
the same literate source (the appendix of the paper).  The input is
Canvas's CSV export of the quiz results; the item titles are the
column keys.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import csv
import sys

<<the item titles are the column keys>>
<<tabulate the closed items>>
<<write the coding sheet>>
<<analysis main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

The item titles are the join keys between the two tangled files: if a
title changes in the quiz, this dictionary is the only other place to
change---and both live in the same source chunk sequence, side by
side.

<<the item titles are the column keys>>=
STUDENT = "name"
CLOSED_ITEMS = ["Python", "How you learned", "Confidence"]
OPEN_ITEMS = ["Previous programming", "Misbehaving program"]
CODING_COLUMNS = ["contrast", "generalisation", "fusion", "notes"]
@

Tabulating a closed item is counting its answer frequencies; we print
one table per item.

<<tabulate the closed items>>=
def tabulate(rows, item):
    """Print the answer frequencies for one closed item."""
    counts = collections.Counter(row[item] for row in rows)
    print(f"\n{item}")
    for answer, count in counts.most_common():
        print(f"  {answer or '(no answer)'}: {count}")
@

The coding sheet holds the open-text answers next to empty columns for
the manual variation-thinking codes; the coder fills in whether the
described debugging exhibits contrast, generalisation or fusion
thinking (\cref{analysis}).

<<write the coding sheet>>=
def write_coding_sheet(rows, path):
    """Write open-text answers with empty coding columns to path."""
    fields = [STUDENT] + OPEN_ITEMS + CODING_COLUMNS
    with open(path, "w", newline="") as out:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=fields, extrasaction="ignore")
        writer.writeheader()
        for row in rows:
            writer.writerow({key: row.get(key, "") for key in fields})
@

The main function ties the pieces together.

<<analysis main>>=
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("results", help="CSV export of the quiz results")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--coding-sheet",
        default="quiz-coding-sheet.csv",
        help="output CSV for manual coding of the open-text answers",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args()

    with open(args.results, newline="") as infile:
        rows = list(csv.DictReader(infile))
    if not rows:
        sys.exit("no rows in the results export")

    print(f"{len(rows)} students")
    for item in CLOSED_ITEMS:
        tabulate(rows, item)
    write_coding_sheet(rows, args.coding_sheet)
    print(f"\ncoding sheet written to {args.coding_sheet}")
@
